WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.900
CHRIS HAAS: Welcome to The
New Era of Service Delivery:

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.790
COVID-19 Digital Transformation.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.530
Welcome, everyone, and thank
you for attending our session.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.520
I hope everyone has been doing
well in these difficult times.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.400
Today we'll be discussing
how the COVID-19 pandemic has

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.740
caused government agencies
to digitally transform

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.320
in how they deliver critical
public health and benefit

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.710
program services
leveraging Google Cloud.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.380
My name is Chris Haas, and I'm
a strategic business executive

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.590
with Google Cloud, focusing
on our public health response

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.640
solutions.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.978
Brandon?

00:00:36.978 --> 00:00:38.770
BRANDON WILLIAMS: Hi,
I'm Brandon Williams.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.680
I'm the innovation
strategy manager

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.410
in Eagle County, Colorado, and I
break things and sometimes even

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.980
fix them.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.420
CHRIS HAAS: Great.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.180
The COVID-19 pandemic
is impacting communities

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.290
all over the world.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.690
At the same time, it has
inspired coordination

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.280
between public and
private sectors

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.730
and across international
borders on a remarkable scale.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.700
The best way for technology
companies like Google to help

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.730
is by supporting the work
of public health authorities

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.460
to keep people healthy and safe.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.370
The full impact of COVID-19
is still unfolding.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.410
However, there are
already major changes

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.840
in the government and the
use of technology in aiding

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.670
pandemic response efforts.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.580
Google is proud to be one of
many organizations partnering

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.808
with government agencies
during this period.

00:01:23.808 --> 00:01:25.600
We are working alongside
government leaders

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.550
to implement new
solutions that help

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.120
them to enable remote workforce
to continue to operate normally

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.520
during the pandemic,
enable them to leverage

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.550
real-time and historical
data for pandemic policy

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:40.180
decision-making, and support
them for what's coming next

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.540
as our country reopens and
the economy jump starts,

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:47.130
including planning for future
phases of the pandemic.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:51.250
The developing silver lining
is how these paradigm shifts

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.380
are affecting change
in how governments

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:59.060
deliver digital services, both
now and beyond the pandemic.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.690
Google is helping governments
in a few major areas--

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.360
aiding agencies to handle
the rapid surge by providing

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.150
scalable systems and
virtual assistance,

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.850
enabling rapid application
development and deployment

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.710
of modern systems to manage
the pandemic benefit programs,

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:17.300
and implementation can
happen within days and weeks

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.590
versus months and years.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.850
Also, leveraging AI and ML for
processing documents to help

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.590
automate the massive
amounts of paper

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.080
being submitted to agencies.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.810
Our differentiator is
our speed and agility

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:32.630
to help the agencies bring
new cost-effective solutions

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:36.170
online, enabling states
to more effectively manage

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.750
constituent applications.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.720
I feel there's one standout
theme in how our country is

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.320
managing this pandemic.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.478
It's the speed of
our response efforts.

00:02:45.478 --> 00:02:47.270
Local governments are
needing to break down

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.660
traditional processes and
modernize legacy systems

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.390
to ensure they are moving
as quickly as possible

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.820
to respond to the
rapidly expanding needs

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.790
of our constituents.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.650
There is an expectation
of government

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.220
to operate as normal even
in these abnormal times.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.650
The vision for our public
health response platform

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.500
is to transform the ability
of public health officials

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.040
to detect, evaluate,
react to, and manage

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.480
risks posed to public health
and safety by the pandemic.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.300
We strive to offer a 10x
scale public health officials

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.820
ability to gain
situational awareness

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.060
across their entire environment
and to effectively engage

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.940
and educate the public at
multiple levels, whether it

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.940
be a specific individual
in contact tracing efforts

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.650
and/or to the public at large.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.290
Public health authorities have
an incredibly important role

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.300
in responding to
COVID-19, but they

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.180
face many challenges due to
the scale of the pandemic.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.820
In addition to organizational
limitations caused

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.180
by legacy processes and
systems which slow the response

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.460
efforts, there is
a flood of new data

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.020
but limited ability
to understand it

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.360
for decision-making,
if they are even

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.480
able to access and leverage it
at all due to regulatory and/or

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.310
privacy concerns.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.950
And their operating environment
is constantly changing,

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.970
resulting in incorrect
or misinformation.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.480
But there are many
opportunities for innovation.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.900
By leveraging Google
Cloud's serverless Analytics

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.510
and AI/ML technology,
government agencies

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.320
are scaling their limited human
resources through automation

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.460
and reducing manual tasks to
identify and trace new cases,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.530
predict the spread of the
virus, and effectively

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.080
engage and educate the public
on the response efforts

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.050
and real-time information.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.050
We need to remember that data
warehousing is not a use case.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.880
Data warehouse is
a solution that

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.640
enables higher order use
cases to make organizations

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.020
data-driven.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.660
Organizations have started
using data warehouses

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.040
as the foundation for
operational reporting.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.610
For example, monthly
standardized reports.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.190
Government organizations must
increasingly enable their teams

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.840
to carry out ad hoc analyses.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.130
This requires the use
of self-service tools.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:58.440
Organizations are
also looking to mine

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.140
the right data stored
in their data warehouses

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.470
to perform ad hoc analysis
to understand correlations,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.410
identify patterns,
and detect anomalies.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.780
As organizations increasingly
aspire to be data-driven,

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.080
they are trying to harness
the rich data in their data

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.080
warehouse to create
predictive analytic models

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.840
and operationalize these
predictive insights.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.750
This requires the data warehouse
to support their machine

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.370
learning initiatives.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.650
Our goal is to help
government transform

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:30.110
into operationalized
decision-making organizations.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:31.830
But the typical data
warehouse project

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.480
is fraught with
failure and millions

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.370
of dollars of wasted money.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.140
Traditional platforms
are just not

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.020
capable of keeping up with
the new demands we have

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.270
on interconnected data needs.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.950
At Google, we are
uniquely positioned

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:47.610
to support organizations in
migrating to new data analytics

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.330
platform technologies.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:49.982
Why?

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.690
Because we used to
have the same problems

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.760
many enterprises now face.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.180
We solve the data pipeline
inefficiencies and complexities

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.640
with a petabyte-scale
data warehouse platform

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.040
called Google Cloud BigQuery.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.800
We offer the same analytics
technology to all enterprises

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.100
now with simple pricing.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.060
BigQuery is fully
managed and serverless,

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.520
which means there is no
infrastructure to manage.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.830
You can simply focus
on analyzing your data

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.845
to find meaningful insights
using familiar SQL queries.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.360
And through our serverless
data analytic solution,

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.810
we eliminate the need for
systems engineering in the data

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.530
Analytics workflow so that you
can focus 100% of your time

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:33.112
on data analysis.

00:06:33.112 --> 00:06:34.570
Governments realize
this innovation

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:37.780
by digitally transforming
through their use of the Google

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.060
public health response platform.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:41.740
Working closely
with our partners,

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.320
Google has built a serverless
analytics and application

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.750
modernization
platform which aids

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.020
public health across major
pandemic response areas,

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.610
such as disease and
case management,

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.360
including active and passive
contact tracing, which

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:59.380
is a critical aspect of public
health pandemic response,

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:02.560
also tracking of
resources and logistics,

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.960
and engaging the community
through proactive outreach

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.340
and education.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.680
At the center of Google
Smart Analytics platform,

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:11.590
enabling government agencies to
leverage their data in new ways

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:15.307
to accelerate innovation and
deliver critical services.

00:07:15.307 --> 00:07:17.140
Integrating public
health response solutions

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.090
with Google's Smart
Analytics platform

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:22.390
enables customers to
easily scale the processing

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.030
and analysis of data.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.440
Our fully managed serverless
approach automatically

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:29.380
takes care of performance,
scalability, and availability

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:32.230
requirements for
your data analysis.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.000
With no infrastructure
to manage,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.760
you can easily
leverage products like

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.350
BigQuery to analyze
gigabytes to petabytes

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:41.350
of data in minutes, not months.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.200
Public health agencies are
now driving instant insight

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.230
from their data, empowering
professional and citizen data

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.950
analysts with AI-powered
predictive insights.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.910
With a secure and
compliant foundation,

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.110
agency constituent data
is secured and private.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.540
Google offers built-in
data protection at scale

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:04.030
to help protect governments from
intrusion, theft, and attacks.

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.750
Our multi-layer
security approach

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:09.230
across hardware services,
user identity, storage,

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:11.430
internet communication
and operations

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:13.160
provides redundancy
and reliability,

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.570
while our purpose-built chips,
servers, storage, network,

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:20.150
and data centers protect against
hardware-level intrusion.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:23.610
Data is automatically encrypted
while in transit and at rest

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.720
and can only be accessed by the
authorized roles and services

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.270
defined by the customer
with audited access

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.280
to the encryption keys.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.020
Within Google
Cloud, data is also

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:35.150
automatically
replicated and encrypted

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.470
for backup and
disaster recovery.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.500
And at a basic level
in public health,

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.929
we can't even begin to have a
conversation without talking

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:43.950
about security and compliance.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.040
So compliance, such as
FedRAMP, HITRUST, and HIPAA

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:49.550
certifications,
amongst others, are

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:52.317
built into the core of
all of our products.

00:08:52.317 --> 00:08:53.900
No matter where you
are in the journey

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.790
to digital transformation,
know that we

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:59.000
have the capabilities to help
you solve your mission outcome.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.810
Using maturity
framework such as this,

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.610
we will help you assess
your organization's maturity

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.740
and serve as your trusted
advisor in identifying

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.870
the appropriate
capabilities and use cases

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:11.270
you should be focusing on.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.500
So let's talk
through an example.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:16.060
Leveraging serverless and
AI-powered Contact Center

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.170
and chat bot solutions, our
Contact Center AI, or CCAI,

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:21.850
and Dialogflow
solutions are rapidly

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:24.670
deployable and able to
integrate with public health

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:27.880
contact tracing processes
to automate the human tracer

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:32.170
activities and reduce manual
case management tasks,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.670
whether that be
engaging with people

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.320
via the phone or online chat.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.640
Leveraging CCAI,
government agencies

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:41.310
can transform their
Contact Center

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:44.160
into an AI-powered
customer-centric optimization

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.160
platform which integrates
all valuable data,

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.530
provides real-time and
relevant information to callers

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.080
and agents, and turns
customer interactions

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.810
into actionable insights.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.310
Here we have an example video.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:57.363
Let's watch for a moment.

00:09:57.363 --> 00:09:58.030
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:00.530
- First, we have a phone call
with a person which the Public

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.880
Health Agency would
like to engage

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.560
with for contact tracing.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:09.070
Powered by Contact
Center AI and Dialogflow,

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:11.500
the virtual agent is able
to interact with a caller

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.310
to collect some of the needed
contact tracing details.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:18.365
Let's have a listen.

00:10:18.365 --> 00:10:23.840
[PHONE RINGING]

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:24.470
- Hi.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.390
This is the
Department of Health.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:30.570
As part of an investigation into
a confirmed case of COVID-19,

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.300
we are following
up with individuals

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.130
who may have had
contact with a case

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.720
while they were
possibly contagious.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.440
We are calling to check on you
and discuss some public health

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.150
recommendations.

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:43.316
Is it OK to go ahead?

00:10:43.316 --> 00:10:46.040
- Yes.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:46.640
- Thank you.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.660
We'll start with your name.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.470
Please tell me your
first and last name.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:52.415
- Brian McDonald.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:55.320
- OK.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:59.370
I have your first name is Brian
and your last name is McDonald.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:02.190
Next, I'll need you to
provide your date of birth.

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:06.240
You can say something like
January 3rd, 2000 or May 15th,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:08.170
1995.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.810
Please go ahead.

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:15.150
- January 17th, 2000.

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:15.970
- Got it.

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:19.650
I have your date of birth
as January 17th, 2000.

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.230
Now, please tell me
your home address.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:28.960
- 1951 East Spring
Street, Jacksonville.

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:31.810
- I have your address
as 1951 East Spring

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:33.760
Street in Jacksonville.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.680
Are you calling in with
your own phone number?

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:39.180
- Yes.

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:39.910
- OK.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:42.390
Right now, we are following
up with individuals

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.210
who may have been present
at John's Bar and Restaurant

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:47.940
on the 24th of May, 2020.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.460
Were you present at the
location on that day?

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:52.986
- I was.

00:11:52.986 --> 00:11:55.770
- A per--

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:59.512
- Next, the caller is
transferred to a live agent.

00:11:59.512 --> 00:12:06.660
[NO AUDIO]

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.020
- --well-being.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.430
Do you have any questions?

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:14.430
- Yes, I want to talk
to a contact tracer.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.090
- Sure.

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:19.380
Please hold while
I transfer you.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.680
- At this point, the live agent
is able to leverage Contact

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:26.220
Center AI's Agent Assist
technology to provide them with

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:29.430
live and recommended
knowledge-based responses

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:30.660
to the caller's questions.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:34.770
- Hello, Brian.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.510
I see you'd like to speak
with a contact tracer.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:38.970
How can I help you?

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:40.470
- Well, thank you.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:43.170
Could you tell me how my
privacy will be protected?

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:48.970
- That's a great question.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:51.690
First off, we want to
thank you for calling in.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.900
We take this very seriously,
and confidentiality

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.390
of your identity and
health information

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.910
is of the utmost importance.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.360
Your name will never be shared
with anyone [INAUDIBLE] to,

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.170
and their privacy will
be protected as well.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:08.880
Ultimately, privacy
encourages participation

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.580
in adoption, and
as a result, will

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:13.916
help us protect more people.

00:13:13.916 --> 00:13:15.305
- Oh, that's great.

00:13:15.305 --> 00:13:16.700
I see.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:19.640
- Leveraging Google
Cloud's Contact Center AI

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:23.900
and Dialogflow, as well as
expertise in AI and ML built

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.870
into our speech-to-text and
Natural Language processing

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:30.410
solutions, we're able to
automate many of the human

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:32.600
contact tracer tasks.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:35.390
Additionally, we are able
to automatically transform

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:38.750
this conversation from
unstructured to structured data

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.147
for further analysis by
public health agencies.

00:13:42.147 --> 00:13:42.730
[END PLAYBACK]

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:44.688
CHRIS HAAS: Here's the
high-level architecture.

00:13:44.688 --> 00:13:48.550
At each stage, collection,
transformation, analysis,

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:51.580
and visualization, we are
using serverless technology

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:55.690
to deliver a modern and scalable
solution for government.

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:58.060
And this solution provides
powerful out-of-the-box

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:01.420
analyses and visualizations
which enables public health

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:05.550
responders to take
action immediately.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.550
Throughout the pandemic,
we've been implementing

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.040
this and many other public
health response solutions

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:12.330
with government customers
around the world.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.970
For example, the
state of Illinois

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:17.540
has deployed CCAI and Dialogflow
in their contact centers

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:20.510
to aid in their pandemic
response efforts,

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:25.260
both for contact tracing
and for benefit programs.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.200
Additionally, the state of
New York has done similar.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.180
Both of these states have
been hit hard by COVID-19

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:35.380
and are seeing success using
these types of solutions

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:38.660
in responding to the pandemic.

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:40.610
So circling back to
our functional overview

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:44.080
and the core Google Cloud
Smart Analytics platform,

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.410
let's take a quick look at
the visualization output

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:48.880
from this platform.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.240
Here's an example.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:55.440
Here we're leveraging
Google's Looker dashboarding,

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:59.980
visualization, and
business intelligence tool

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:05.720
to bring in, visualize, and
analyze all of the COVID-19

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:09.950
data that we're collecting
into the public health response

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:11.280
platform.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:14.000
And remember, this as a
serverless environment.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.100
The back end is
completely serverless,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:19.970
and the front end dashboards
that we're looking at here

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:23.410
are also running in a
serverless environment.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:26.470
First, we're looking at
the state of Colorado,

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:30.940
all counties, and all
data year-to-date,

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:38.510
and we're scaling it to just
the raw data population size.

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:41.740
Now we're focusing it on
the last two weeks only,

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:53.210
and we're scaling
it to 100,000 cases.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.750
And we're able to drill down
even further into county level

00:15:56.750 --> 00:16:01.120
data, and we're also able to
bring in many other public data

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:05.270
sets available within
Google's BigQuery solution,

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:10.980
including mobility, weather,
and other COVID-19 public data

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:11.480
sets.

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:23.180
All of this is delivered
to our customers

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:26.690
through low-maintenance
serverless solutions

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:30.680
so that you can
focus on analyzing

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:35.060
the data to make decisions
versus maintaining

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:36.796
the underlying infrastructure.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:46.980
To deliver this type of
solution, as I've said before,

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:48.660
we use our Smart
Analytics platform,

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.010
with the core of
that being BigQuery.

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:54.740
BigQuery brings in
native capabilities

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:58.790
that allow you to do both
petabyte-scale analysis

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:01.220
in addition to integrated
machine learning

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.110
and GIS functionality.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:05.630
And it is a truly
serverless platform

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:09.990
that allows you to focus solely
on your analytical needs.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:13.079
Additionally, for
application modernization,

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:16.380
you can leverage Anthos,
which is our application

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.599
modernization
infrastructure that

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:21.150
can be used in a
multicloud environment

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.970
to deliver these
type of capabilities.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.670
So with that, I'd like to
hand it over to Brandon.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:28.517
He'll take it from here.

00:17:28.517 --> 00:17:29.850
BRANDON WILLIAMS: Thanks, Chris.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:32.010
Yeah, I think you
nailed it in talking

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:36.240
about the need for
public sector employees

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:38.940
to be able to do
complex analysis using

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:41.790
simple tools Natural Language.

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:44.790
We started on the arc
at the very lowest level

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.010
when COVID first
started spreading.

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:49.902
We are a international
ski destination.

00:17:49.902 --> 00:17:52.360
And you can bet, if somebody
sneezes anywhere in the world,

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:55.050
we're probably going to
see it here at some point.

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:56.290
COVID was no different.

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:57.930
We saw it pretty early on.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:00.870
Now, the complexity here
was we had limited testing.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.900
Despite what was being
said, the test volume

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:05.470
was very, very limited.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:08.310
We knew we had
community spread, and we

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:12.708
knew, because recovery planning
starts when an incident starts,

00:18:12.708 --> 00:18:14.250
we were going to
have to build trust.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:18.750
And in a pandemic, the
public is just as much a team

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:22.110
player as anybody sitting on
the first responder or emergency

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:23.470
operation centers.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.150
They may serve in
different capacities,

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:27.210
but we knew that
trust and data sharing

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:32.190
was going to be key to being
able to get through this.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:34.560
So the first thing we
did was very simple.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:37.760
We used a Google Form, and we
sat down with our EPI teams,

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:38.900
and we got it out the door.

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:40.730
And this was really
just tracking symptoms.

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:43.300
Where we couldn't do testing,
we wanted to know who was sick

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:44.900
and who was not feeling well.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.300
So we made it
mobile-friendly, Google Forms,

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:52.990
dumped into a Sheet, and we were
able to tie it to Data Studio.

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:55.910
The Data Studio allowed us to
take the information regarding

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:58.250
symptoms, the stuff
outside of testing,

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.350
and combine it with the
stuff that we were getting

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.600
from our private
medical practitioners

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:04.970
and other nonprofit
and other partners

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:08.180
and start to build a 360
picture of what our community

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.220
impact was really looking like.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:13.820
It was important to us early on
to only create one dashboard.

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:15.800
We didn't create a
community dashboard and then

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:18.760
an internal
operations dashboard.

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:20.990
There's one dashboard,
and so we automated

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:24.260
as much as possible to make
sure information we were getting

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:29.500
was going right out the door
to the public in real-time.

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:32.240
And so, from that, we
got a lot of questions

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.000
from other communities on
how they could replicate it.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:36.860
So we stood up a
quick Google Site.

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:40.460
We spent some time with
YouTube, created some templates,

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:43.250
and we made it available,
widely available to anybody

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:44.410
that wanted it.

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:46.910
And very quickly we saw
it adopted across counties

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:50.714
in Colorado and nationwide.

00:19:50.714 --> 00:19:52.430
Now, that turned into--

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:55.470
thankfully, we've got a really
close working relationship

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.030
with the state of
Colorado anyway.

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.760
They've been a longtime
Google partner as we have,

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:03.390
and so being able to share
data in Google was natural.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:07.230
And this spread up to
the governor's office

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.960
and across to the departments
to be able to figure out,

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:11.910
how could we do
symptoms tracking

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.490
and share the information
back to locals

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:18.090
in a very narrow sense
so that it was secure,

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.010
real-time information
for tactical level

00:20:21.010 --> 00:20:23.730
at the local level so that
they could maneuver resources

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:27.150
and respond, but give the
state that strategic overview

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:28.680
and picture that
they needed to be

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.585
able to start
allocating and thinking

00:20:30.585 --> 00:20:31.710
next steps in the response?

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:36.300
And so we worked
together with the state

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.010
and a lot of other
partners, and we

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.920
were very happy to be able to
launch the same kind of symptom

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:43.800
tracker we did in Eagle
County at the state level

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.230
to start to get
that 360 picture.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:49.620
And that's turned into a much
wider discussion about how

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:52.980
we share data across the
disease prevention and disease

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:54.535
monitoring spectrum.

00:20:57.520 --> 00:21:00.600
And so, specifically, it's
created a new normal of data

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:01.770
sharing.

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:05.280
We're starting to look at that
tactical and strategic levels,

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:08.220
being able to dump information
into central locations

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.410
so that we can
throw BigQuery at it

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:13.770
and extract the data in
Natural Language that's

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:17.580
unique to a geography
or a demographic need

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.710
and be able to share
that information securely

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.085
across a vast
number of partners.

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:27.740
But it hasn't been just Google
Forms and Sheets and Data

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:28.580
Studio.

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.970
We've seen an explosion in all
of the Office productivity tool

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:32.720
set.

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:33.950
Meet, unsurprising.

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:36.350
We used to be able to
get together and talk.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:39.200
Now we have to do it remotely,
and so our data regarding

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:41.210
Meet explosion is unsurprising.

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:44.390
Where I think it is surprising
is in the changing adoption

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:47.900
rates that we saw regarding
Groups, regarding Maps,

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:51.810
and other dashboard
elements, Charts and Sheets.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:53.690
And specifically
around Google Sites,

00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:55.850
we've seen a real
explosion in stuff

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:59.240
we would have generated
in PDF or other reports,

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.060
we're now doing
dynamically in Sites.

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.060
And this is an example.

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:11.180
Here, we had a regional response
where we had our COVID testing

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:15.050
locations coming off of Google
Sheets, some other data.

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:17.150
We created a
mobile-friendly Google Site

00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:19.370
that's simple enough
for my mom to use,

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:23.410
and we pack it in with a
map and contact information.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:26.780
And that's updated
in near real-time so

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:29.090
that all of the
testing information

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:32.273
and how to initiate that process
is immediately accessible.

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:39.100
It's carried over into some of
the Just-In-Time training we've

00:22:39.100 --> 00:22:41.260
had to do for emergency
operations center.

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:44.440
A pandemic brings in a lot
of partners, many of whom

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:47.080
have never stepped inside of
an emergency operation center

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:47.960
before.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:49.960
And so to teach them
about incident command,

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:53.620
emergency operations, what their
job functions and roles are,

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:55.360
we stood up a Google Site.

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:57.880
We were able to educate
them with simple YouTube

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:02.170
videos, Google Docs,
Drive aids, job aids,

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:04.450
and be able to convey
information so that they could

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:07.060
be prepared when they first
stepped in to be able to help

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:10.460
and pitch in on the response.

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:13.080
It's helped us look at
our own internal planning.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.860
Here is our equivalent
of an intranet.

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:17.570
As we moved to
remote work, we had

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:19.730
about 170 different
activities that

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:22.310
now needed to take
place without a VPN,

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:24.960
direct on any device securely.

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.620
So we moved a lot of
our processes to Google,

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:29.900
and now all of our
internal operations

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:32.870
are accessible within a
single landing page that's

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:34.430
greatly reduced complexity.

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:36.770
And it's given us
additional visibility

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:40.240
into usage, who's doing what,
what are the needs, where

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:42.200
are we seeing surges,
and being able to respond

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:45.410
to that accordingly.

00:23:45.410 --> 00:23:47.270
I think one of the
more exciting things,

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:51.050
and you alluded to it
earlier, was Dialogflow.

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:55.160
Dialogflow in a pandemic, we've
got 40 or 50 different players.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:57.560
Everybody is going
so-and-so deep.

00:23:57.560 --> 00:24:01.470
The information fluctuates
on a constant basis.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.020
And we needed one
way that people

00:24:03.020 --> 00:24:05.930
could Natural Language
search to get information

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:07.310
that was important to them.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:09.590
Dialogflow is a perfect fit.

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.930
In this case, in the
low code environment,

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:14.150
it took us eight days
from the first time

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:16.460
we sat around a table
talking about it to where

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.410
we had something deployed.

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:20.820
Was a very, very
quick turnaround.

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:24.800
And now we're able to respond by
still keeping Google Docs where

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:28.340
the primary players are updating
their information in real-time.

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:31.100
We scrape that and make
it available in the chat.

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:33.140
We've expanded
that beyond COVID.

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:34.820
As we're in wildfire
season, we're

00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:38.360
now addressing fire restrictions
data, wildfire response

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:39.283
information.

00:24:39.283 --> 00:24:40.700
And we're now on
a path that we're

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.710
going to bring all of
our government services

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:44.605
in so that you
can get to a point

00:24:44.605 --> 00:24:46.730
where you can ask your
fridge whether your driver's

00:24:46.730 --> 00:24:48.770
license is up for renewal.

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:50.630
I think this new normal
has brought together

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.160
a lot of lessons.

00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:53.960
I'm really excited about--

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:56.150
I wish we would have
done it in an exercise,

00:24:56.150 --> 00:25:00.860
but I am really excited about
the wider inclusion steps

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:03.200
that we've taken
horizontally and vertically

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:06.410
across local governments with
state government and this data

00:25:06.410 --> 00:25:08.720
sharing effort.

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:11.810
I think keeping it
simple was important.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:14.600
Now that all of our budgets
just got obliterated,

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.480
being able to track cost
avoidance and efficiencies

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:20.650
with tools that we already
have at our fingertips,

00:25:20.650 --> 00:25:24.150
our staff has been on Google
for, I think, eight years now,

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:26.750
and so these were tools that
they naturally stepped into,

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:28.160
immediately deployed.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:31.430
And most importantly, the
tools like that Google Form

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:33.680
for symptoms tracking,
we deployed that

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:35.720
within those
initial first hours.

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:38.510
And those were
designed by myself,

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:41.660
and the lead of the EPI team
sat down and hammered it out.

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.300
So there was no delay in
waiting for communications

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:46.020
or other designers to step in.

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:46.670
We designed it.

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:48.370
We deployed it.

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:50.690
And so I think that's really
key to moving forward--

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:51.980
deploying and iterating.

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:53.690
Being able to do
something like the chat

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:55.310
bot in real-time
where information

00:25:55.310 --> 00:25:56.380
is changing so often.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:59.000
And keeping that updated
with a lot of partners

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:02.780
has been invaluable in
coordinating our response.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:05.030
Here, making sure
the public had access

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:07.960
transparent, accountable,
in real-time,

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:10.760
and being able to deliver
that in very short order

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:11.860
has been important.

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:14.690
Government sharing
is just too hard.

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:16.940
We need these recipe
books, a library

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:18.620
of customizable templates.

00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:22.220
We've seen an explosion in
sharing across government

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:25.220
sectors to be able to
contribute to each other

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.170
to help in the COVID
response, and I'm really

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:29.700
excited to see where that
goes in the next step.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.354
I am a true believer, and I do
believe government's awesome.

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:37.720
I think the future of
government work, like everybody,

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:40.540
remote work has now been
proven to be a reality.

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:42.790
So shifting into these
collaboration tools that

00:26:42.790 --> 00:26:46.900
provide the same security access
on any device from anywhere

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:49.390
and being able to work
confidently remotely

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:52.420
and do so confident that
your information is secure

00:26:52.420 --> 00:26:57.550
has been pervasive in every
single program that we do.

00:26:57.550 --> 00:26:59.290
I'm really charged
up about the way

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:01.690
that leadership
has embraced this.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:04.570
Seeing government
leaders think creatively

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:06.520
about using these
collaboration tools

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:08.410
and demanding things
like Dashboard

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:14.050
to make data-driven decisions
and change deployment

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:17.050
practices, sometimes even
in the middle of a response,

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.170
to be able to respond to those
community needs, I think,

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:22.090
is really exciting.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:25.720
From a collaboration standpoint,
this has been unprecedented.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.330
Having private sector
nonprofits and others

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:30.430
sitting in that emergency
operations center

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:32.470
and sharing
real-time information

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.690
to make sure economic
recovery is happening,

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:37.020
the community needs are
being taken care of,

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.840
and that we're prioritizing
cases for testing and handling

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:42.520
has been exciting.

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:46.840
I'm looking forward to where
that takes us in future efforts

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:51.490
when it's not a pandemic, and
I think low cost, high impact.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:54.430
Nothing demonstrates
this better than being

00:27:54.430 --> 00:27:57.640
able to look at the impact
that we're taking financially

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.733
from the lack of things,
like travel revenue,

00:28:00.733 --> 00:28:02.650
and trying to project
what that impact's going

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:04.320
to be over the next
couple of years,

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.610
and moving into that
with a confidence

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.950
that we have a toolset that's
going to enable us to be

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:12.070
able to respond with the
same type of delivery

00:28:12.070 --> 00:28:15.340
that the customer expects
in their private life,

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:17.830
and expecting government
to move at the speed

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:20.140
of the private sector,
and now we can do so.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:23.110
I think one of the things
that's really come out of this

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:26.710
is that need for public sector
communities to come together.

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:29.680
And I'm so proud of the work
that we're doing with Google,

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:32.110
with our partners in the
UK, with our partners

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:35.440
at the state, local, and federal
level here in the United States

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:37.720
where we've launched a public
sector community that's

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:42.370
building those cookbooks,
those recipe books for copy,

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:45.670
customize, and deploy, no
additional cost, and the tools

00:28:45.670 --> 00:28:47.500
that you already use.

00:28:47.500 --> 00:28:49.600
And that and just
the conversations

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:51.910
that are having it, bringing
a face to the common

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:54.880
challenges that we have, and
we meet on a regular basis now.

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:57.460
And I'm looking forward
to bringing so many more

00:28:57.460 --> 00:29:00.518
members in with so many more
different ideas and eyes.

00:29:00.518 --> 00:29:01.810
CHRIS HAAS: Thank you, Brandon.

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:04.730
It's always a pleasure
to partner with you.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:06.730
You're a great
person to work with,

00:29:06.730 --> 00:29:08.470
a great team that
you have up there,

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:10.240
and thank you again
for presenting

00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:12.093
with me in this session.

00:29:12.093 --> 00:29:13.260
BRANDON WILLIAMS: Thank you.

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:15.160
Looking forward to it.

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:17.150
CHRIS HAAS: Thanks, everyone.

